==English==

===Etymology===
From {{der|en|frm|laps}}, from {{der|en|la|lāpsus}}, from {{m|la|lābī||to slip}}. {{doublet|en|lapsus}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|en|/læps/|a=UK,US}}
* {{audio|en|LL-Q1860 (eng)-Naomi Persephone Amethyst (NaomiAmethyst)-lapse.wav|a=GA}}
* {{audio|en|LL-Q1860 (eng)-Vealhurl-lapse.wav|a=Southern England}}
* {{rhymes|en|æps|s=1}}
* {{homophones|en|laps}}

===Noun===
{{en-noun}}

# A [[temporary]] [[failure]]; a [[slip]].
#: {{syn|en|blooper|gaffe|thinko|Thesaurus:error}}
#: {{coi|en|memory '''lapse'''}}
#: {{coi|en|'''lapse''' of judgment}}
#: {{coi|en|'''lapse''' in security}}
#: {{coi|en|'''lapse''' in concentration}}
#: {{ux|en|It was a regrettable '''lapse''' by a person of otherwise exemplary character.}}
# A [[decline]] or [[fall]] in [[standard]]s.
#* {{quote-journal|en|date=September 10, 1751|author=Samuel Johnson|title=No. CLV|magazine=w:The Rambler|oclc=29126588|passage=The '''lapse''' to indolence is soft and imperceptible, because it is only a mere cessation of activity}}
# A [[pause]] in [[continuity]].
#: {{syn|en|hiatus|moratorium|Thesaurus:pause}}
# An [[interval]] of [[time]] between [[event]]s.
#: {{syn|en|between-time|gap|Thesaurus:interim}}
#* {{RQ:Tennyson In Memoriam|XXVI|43|Still onward winds the dreary way;<br>⁠I with it; for I long to prove<br>⁠No '''lapse''' of moons can canker Love,<br>Whatever fickle tongues may say.}}
#* {{RQ:Isaac Taylor Ultimate Civilization|page=309|passage=Bacon was content to wait the '''lapse''' of long centuries for his expected revenue of fame}}
# A [[termination]] of a [[right]] etc., through [[disuse]] or [[neglect]].
# {{lb|en|meteorology}} A marked decrease in [[air]] [[temperature]] with increasing [[altitude]] because the [[ground]] is warmer than the surrounding air.
# {{lb|en|legal}} A [[common-law]] rule that if the person to whom property is [[will#Verb|will]]ed were to die before the [[testator]], then the gift would be ineffective.
# {{lb|en|theology}} A [[fall]] or [[apostasy]].

